(Toward an O*NET for AI R&D: Proposing a new way to track AI research automation) Tj T*
(Jean-Stanislas Denain, Joe Kwon and Anson Ho, Epoch AI Gradient Updates, 17 June 2026) Tj T*
(Source: https://epoch.ai/gradient-updates/toward-an-onet-for-ai-rnd) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Toward an O*NET for AI R&D) Tj T*
(Newsletter) Tj T*
(Jun. 17, 2026) Tj T*
(Toward an O*NET for AI R&D) Tj T*
(Proposing a new way to track AI research automation) Tj T*
(Cite) Tj T*
(By Jean-Stanislas Denain, Joe Kwon, and Anson Ho) Tj T*
(Gradient Updates shares more opinionated or informal takes on big questions in AI progress.) Tj T*
(These posts solely represent the views of the authors, and do not necessarily reflect the views) Tj T*
(of Epoch AI as a whole.) Tj T*
(What trends are we extrapolating?) Tj T*
(A common way that experts forecast AI timelines is so simple it's hard to believe: trend) Tj T*
(extrapolation. Sure, they also use numerical models that bake in things like runaway feedback) Tj T*
(loops, but the bread and butter of AI forecasting is to draw a line on a graph and extend it as) Tj T*
(far as you dare. Somehow this works well enough to be a state-of-the-art approach. However, the) Tj T*
(trends they extrapolate share a common weakness: they lean heavily on easy-to-measure things,) Tj T*
(not what we directly care about  -  how close AI is to doing AI research itself.) Tj T*
(Many experts want to know when we'll fully automate AI research, because this would massively) Tj T*
(speed up AI progress, kicking off an "intelligence explosion".1 If that's right, it's hugely) Tj T*
(important to know how close we are. But historically, there haven't been many points of direct) Tj T*
(evidence to point to, because full automation of AI R&D has been so hard to measure. Instead,) Tj T*
(researchers have been forced to rely on proxies.) Tj T*
(One such proxy is in key AI inputs like compute, data, and energy. Take Situational Awareness,) Tj T*
(which extends "effective compute" five years into the future until AI research gets automated:) Tj T*
(But these inputs are only rough proxies for capabilities and you need to go on "vibes" to say) Tj T*
(how much compute you need to match the world's top AI researchers.) Tj T*
(A second approach is to track the length of tasks that AIs can complete, where "length" is how) Tj T*
(long a skilled human takes. This is what METR did with their "time horizon" metric, which was) Tj T*
(then extrapolated in the famous AI 2027 scenario to forecast when AIs would become "superhuman) Tj T*
(coders", before automating AI R&D.) Tj T*
(But the benchmarks used to construct METR's time horizon miss out on many of the tasks and) Tj T*
(complexities of research. It's one thing to be able to finetune a small GPT-2 model, and) Tj T*
(another to be able to coordinate five projects at the same time, dealing with a million lines) Tj T*
(of code, without a clear criterion for success or failure.) Tj T*
(So it would be nice to know everything that AI R&D actually consists of. Otherwise we'll always) Tj T*
(be at risk of streetlighting \(extrapolating whatever happens to be easiest to measure\). We can) Tj T*
(better avoid this if we have a list of the tasks involved in AI R&D, to track what we're aware) Tj T*
(of and what we're not investigating.) Tj T*
(Armed with such a list, we could better interpret the extrapolations we already have: when a) Tj T*
(benchmark score climbs, a task list tells us which parts of the job that improvement covers,) Tj T*
(and which parts it doesn't touch. And we could build new trends to extrapolate, like the) Tj T*
(fraction of tasks that are automated, or how much uplift researchers get on each one.) Tj T*
(In this post, we present a first version of such a taxonomy with six categories spanning a) Tj T*
(frontier AI company's research workflow, based on literature review and brainstorming. The) Tj T*
(categories are broken into over sixty tasks, each rated from 0 to 5 on how much we think) Tj T*
(current AIs automate it. The full taxonomy lives in a companion doc, which is the main artifact) Tj T*
(of this work.) Tj T*
(We think of it as our best first attempt at developing a comprehensive taxonomy of tasks) Tj T*
ET
endstream
