BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 54 738 Td
(contributing to AI R&D, in order to more robustly understand and predict AI R&D automation. If) Tj T*
(we described tasks incorrectly, or missed some entirely, we'd love to hear about it and receive) Tj T*
(feedback.) Tj T*
(Get the latest from Epoch AISubscribe) Tj T*
(An O*NET for AI R&D) Tj T*
(We're not the first to want a task list like this; breaking jobs down into tasks is how) Tj T*
(economists usually track automation in the economy. The standard tool here is O*NET, which) Tj T*
(describes about 1,000 jobs in the US economy as well as the tasks and skills that people need) Tj T*
(to do them.) Tj T*
(Armed with O*NET, economists can then do a bunch of empirical work and forecasts about AI's) Tj T*
(economic impacts, such as:) Tj T*
(Look at the fraction of American workers whose jobs might be heavily impacted by LLMs) Tj T*
(Estimate how AI might impact GDP growth over the next decade \(however accurately or) Tj T*
(inaccurately\)) Tj T*
(Design AI benchmarks to cover a wide range of knowledge work) Tj T*
(Taxonomize how people use frontier AI models) Tj T*
(Predict the consequences of automating remote work) Tj T*
(But while O*NET is a widely used source, it doesn't help us track AI research automation very) Tj T*
(well  -  the tasks in the dataset are just way too broad and high-level. Consider the job) Tj T*
("Computer and Information Research Scientists", which is probably about as close as you can get) Tj T*
(to "frontier lab AI researcher" within O*NET. In this case the work tasks look like this:) Tj T*
(The first listed task is to "Analyze problems to develop solutions involving computer hardware) Tj T*
(and software". But that's really vague  -  what kinds of problems or solutions? What exactly) Tj T*
(does or doesn't count as "involving computer hardware and software"? You could make the case) Tj T*
(that almost everything about an AI engineer's job involves computer software, so it's not a) Tj T*
(very granular description to say the least. And yet this is the most granular task description) Tj T*
(you can find in O*NET, and the same issue applies to pretty much every other task.) Tj T*
(Part of the issue here is practical feasibility: O*NET was designed for the ambitious endeavor) Tj T*
(of mapping out the tasks in the entire US economy, before the days of LLMs. So it's probably) Tj T*
(too much to ask for these tasks to be so granular for everything as new and niche as "frontier) Tj T*
(lab AI research".) Tj T*
(That's why our proposal is to build an O*NET for AI R&D specifically. If we keep our focus) Tj T*
(narrow, we're in a much better position to come up with a fine-grained decomposition of an AI) Tj T*
(researcher's job. If this works well, we could look at the fraction of tasks that are automated) Tj T*
(over time. It could also help us interpret benchmark results and how significant they are.) Tj T*
(There's a long-standing phenomenon where AI benchmarks haven't fully reflected the complexities) Tj T*
(of the real world, and so it's important to juxtapose benchmark results with what's happening) Tj T*
(on the ground.) Tj T*
(It could also serve as a framework for additional studies about AI's impact. This could mean) Tj T*
(surveying AI researchers on the uplift they get on different work tasks. It could also mean) Tj T*
(classifying internal AI usage logs into different use cases, like "writing experiment code" or) Tj T*
("deciding what experiment to run next". This is similar to how Anthropic's Clio system helps) Tj T*
(study real-world AI usage while preserving people's privacy.) Tj T*
(More generally, an O*NET for AI R&D would help establish a common vocabulary between) Tj T*
(researchers, such as in frontier labs' model cards. This could help model developers summarize) Tj T*
(where AI does or doesn't help in finer detail, and help standardize information across) Tj T*
(different sources, like in METR's most recent Frontier Risk Report.) Tj T*
(This being said, we're not saying that an "O*NET for AI R&D is all you need" to monitor and) Tj T*
(forecast progress to automation. Even if we're armed with a perfectly comprehensive dataset of) Tj T*
(tasks, we'd still need to measure how AI performs on each task, for example. But even still,) Tj T*
(having a task dataset would help ground the debate about AI's actual impacts on AI research,) Tj T*
(and give us "situational awareness" about how close we might be to an intelligence explosion,) Tj T*
ET
endstream
